{"prdMarkdown":"# PRD: Downloadable PDF Export for PRD and TRD Sessions\n\n## Problem Statement\nCurrently, users can only review generated PRD and TRD documents as rendered markdown text within the browser interface in `public/index.html`. Product managers and engineers frequently need to circulate specs with leadership, clients, or cross-functional stakeholders who prefer standardized, offline-accessible document formats. Manually copying raw markdown or relying on browser print layouts produces inconsistent styling and requires manual formatting work.\n\n## Goals\n- Provide a one-click PDF export action directly on the results view in `public/index.html` alongside the existing PRD/TRD tabs.\n- Generate clean, formatted PDF documents on demand without persisting PDF binary files to storage or the database.\n- Maintain high-fidelity rendering for technical markdown elements such as code snippets, tables, and lists.\n\n## Non-Goals\n- Persistent storage of generated PDF files in PostgreSQL (`src/db/schema.sql`) or cloud object storage.\n- Batch or multi-session PDF exports.\n- In-browser WYSIWYG editing or PDF annotations prior to download.\n\n## User Impact & Persona\n- **Target Users**: Product managers, tech leads, and developers using Spec Clarity to scope features.\n- **User Flow**: Once clarification answers are submitted and documents are generated, an \"Export PDF\" button becomes active next to the PRD/TRD view tabs in `public/index.html`. Clicking it initiates the download.\n- **Access Model**: Anyone with access to the session link will be permitted to trigger an export (assumed, as the creator restriction question was skipped during clarification).\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- `[PRODUCT_DECISION]` Should the export combine both the PRD and TRD into a single PDF document, or provide options to download them as individual PDF files?\n- `[PRODUCT_DECISION]` What document styling, cover page, and metadata (e.g., session timestamp, repository name, page numbers, or table of contents) should be included in the exported PDF?\n- `[ASSUMPTION_MADE]` Access permissions: Assumed that anyone holding the session link can export the PDF, as the clarifying question was skipped.","trdMarkdown":"# TRD: Downloadable PDF Export for PRD and TRD Sessions\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- **Trigger & UI Integration**: A new download action button will be added adjacent to the tab switcher in `public/index.html`. When triggered, it will either call an export route (e.g., `GET /api/sessions/:id/export/pdf`) or trigger a client-side rendering pipeline.\n- **On-Demand Generation**: Per user requirements, PDF generation must be strictly on demand without database persistence or file cache layers in `src/db/index.js` or `src/db/schema.sql`.\n- **Document Pipeline**: The pipeline must consume the markdown strings produced by `src/pipeline/docGenerator.js` (or retrieved from `sessions.prd` and `sessions.trd`), parse them into structured HTML/PDF layout, and stream the resulting PDF buffer with appropriate headers (`Content-Type: application/pdf`, `Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=...`).\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **`package.json` Missing Dependencies**: Currently, `package.json` contains no PDF generation engine (e.g., `pdfkit`, `puppeteer`, `html-pdf-node`) or markdown-to-HTML parser (e.g., `marked`). New dependencies must be introduced. `[confidence: high, grounded: true]`\n- **`Dockerfile` System Dependencies**: If a headless browser approach (e.g., Puppeteer) is chosen for high-fidelity markdown-to-PDF conversion, the lightweight Node container defined in `Dockerfile` will fail unless updated with required Linux shared libraries (e.g., Chromium dependencies, font packages like `fonts-liberation`). `[confidence: high, grounded: true]`\n- **`src/routes/sessions.js` Incomplete Session States**: An export requested before documents have been generated (e.g., while the session is still awaiting clarification answers or running pipeline stages) must be rejected with HTTP 400 or HTTP 409 Conflict instead of generating an empty document. `[confidence: high, grounded: true]`\n- **`public/index.html` Concurrent Triggering**: PDF generation can take several seconds. The UI must disable the export button and show an active loading indicator to prevent users from queueing duplicate generation requests. `[confidence: medium, grounded: true]`\n- **Markdown Syntax Quirks & Pagination**: Complex elements like nested code blocks, wide tables, and blockquotes risk awkward page cutoffs without explicit CSS `@media print` break-inside/page-break rules. `[confidence: medium, grounded: false]`\n- **Resource Exhaustion**: On-demand server-side PDF generation is CPU- and memory-intensive; without rate limiting or concurrency throttling, repeated download requests could degrade the Node server process in `src/server.js`. `[confidence: medium, grounded: false]`\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- `[ENGINEERING_DECISION]` Should PDF generation run server-side in Node.js (requiring heavier libraries like Puppeteer or PDFKit and Docker OS dependencies) or client-side in the browser via `window.print` / DOM-to-PDF libraries to keep the server lightweight?\n- `[ENGINEERING_DECISION]` How should concurrent export requests and resource usage be mitigated if generating PDFs server-side (e.g., in-memory concurrency queue, request rate limiting, or payload size limits)?\n\n## Estimate\n- **Baseline**: 2-3 days\n- **Worst Case**: Up to 7 days (if headless browser containerization in `Dockerfile`, print CSS tuning, and memory management require extensive configuration)"}